<%# Questions about the document, laid out as a conversation: the reader's own
    words on the right, the answer on the left (D4). No avatars and no
    timestamps — a time would have to be rendered in the server's zone, which is
    the very thing the retention countdown exists to avoid (D4, R2.7).

    Attribution is text, not colour or position, so it survives a screen reader
    and a monochrome display alike (R2.6). The list stays an <ol> for the same
    reason: the order of an exchange is meaning, not decoration.

    The whole exchange and its input sit on one filled panel, so the chat reads
    as a place rather than as a form that happens to follow a transcript. The
    fill is the boundary, which is why there is no border: `rule` is only
    3.45:1 on paper, so a border drawn on a tinted surface would sit below the
    3:1 that non-text contrast requires.

    The list is always rendered, empty or not, because the Turbo Stream that
    answers a question appends to it. A list that only exists once it has
    contents is a list the first answer cannot be appended to.

    Answers carry where they came from, so a reader can check rather than take
    them on trust. %>
<section aria-labelledby="ask-heading" class="mb-8 rounded-2xl bg-highlight-bg p-5 sm:p-8">
  <h2 id="ask-heading" class="text-lead font-bold mb-5">Ask about this document</h2>
  <hr>
  <% if document.messages.none? %>
    <p id="no-questions" class="text-body text-ink-muted mb-6">
      No questions yet. Ask anything about what this document says.
    </p>
  <% end %>

  <ol id="messages" class="<%= "mb-6" if document.messages.any? %> space-y-4 list-none">
    <%= render partial: "documents/message", collection: document.messages.ordered, as: :message %>
  </ol>

  <%= render "documents/ask_form", document: document %>
</section>
